From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 11:54:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] pex-stun: re-learn the auth port at the start of every
 refresh round

The data port query is sent from the WireGuard port and asks the STUN server
to reply to the auth (PEX) port instead, via RESPONSE-PORT, since WireGuard
owns the data port's socket. Behind a NAT that reply only gets through while
the mapping created by the auth port's own query still exists, which is for
a minute or two of UDP conntrack lifetime. The auth port was queried once at
start-up and never again, so every 15-minute refresh of the data port timed
out and the first value stayed for the lifetime of the process.

That hides a NAT whose mapping has moved. Seen with a gateway that itself
bound UDP 51830 for its own WireGuard: once that socket existed, the
masquerade gave the inner host's 51830 the external port 30827 for every
destination, while unetd kept announcing 51830 from its first measurement.
Peers connected to the announced port and reached the gateway's WireGuard,
which did not know them; the inner host's own initiations left with source
port 30827, which a port-restricted NAT on the other side had never seen.

Start each refresh round with the auth query again, so the mapping exists
right before the data query's reply needs it. The round costs one extra
query per server every 15 minutes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019FGFD5nZ9AL3AdMi7SfYSg
---
 pex-stun.c | 16 ++++++++++++++--
 pex.h      |  2 ++
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/pex-stun.c b/pex-stun.c
index 564ca71..05ceefa 100644
--- a/pex-stun.c
+++ b/pex-stun.c
@@ -145,7 +145,7 @@ network_stun_query_next(struct network *net)
 	if (ep.sa.sa_family != AF_INET || !ep.in.sin_port)
 		goto out;
 
-	if (!stun->wgport_disabled && stun->auth_port_ext)
+	if (!stun->wgport_disabled && stun->auth_port_ext && !stun->auth_refresh)
 		res_port = stun->auth_port_ext;
 
 	D_NET(net, "Send STUN query to %s, res_port=%d, wg_disabled=%d",
@@ -159,7 +159,8 @@ retry:
 	s->req_auth_port = false;
 	if (stun->wgport_disabled) {
 		ret = sendto(stun->socket.fd, msg, len, 0, &ep.sa, sizeof(ep.in));
-	} else if (!stun->auth_port_ext || pex_raw_socket(AF_INET) < 0) {
+	} else if (!stun->auth_port_ext || stun->auth_refresh ||
+		   pex_raw_socket(AF_INET) < 0) {
 		/*
 		 * Without a raw socket the WireGuard source port cannot be
 		 * forged, so query from the PEX socket instead. That discovers
@@ -239,6 +240,8 @@ void network_stun_rx_packet(struct network *net, const void *data, size_t len)
 		return;
 
 	network_stun_update_port(net, s->req_auth_port, s->req.port);
+	if (s->req_auth_port)
+		stun->auth_refresh = false;
 	/*
 	 * Learning the auth (PEX) port is only the first step towards the
 	 * WireGuard data port, which needs a raw socket to forge its source
@@ -268,6 +271,15 @@ restart:
 		network_stun_close_socket(net);
 		next = 15 * 60 * 1000;
 		stun->state = STUN_STATE_STUN_QUERY_SEND;
+		/*
+		 * The data port query asks the server to reply to the auth
+		 * port (RESPONSE-PORT), so that reply can only pass a NAT while
+		 * the mapping from the auth port's own query still exists.
+		 * That is for a minute or two, not until the next round. Start
+		 * every round with the auth query again, which recreates the
+		 * mapping right before the data query needs it.
+		 */
+		stun->auth_refresh = true;
 		D_NET(net, "STUN idle");
 		break;
 	case STUN_STATE_PEX_QUERY_WAIT:
diff --git a/pex.h b/pex.h
index f0173a6..f1604a4 100644
--- a/pex.h
+++ b/pex.h
@@ -56,6 +56,8 @@ struct network_stun {
 
 	enum network_stun_state state;
 	bool wgport_disabled;
+	/* re-learn the auth port first in the next query round */
+	bool auth_refresh;
 
 	uint16_t auth_port_ext;
 	uint16_t port_local;
